Chevrolet has a long history of producing category-defining vehicles like the Corvette, Silverado, and Camaro. But not every Chevy lives up to that standard.

Over the years, the brand has issued numerous recalls and faced widespread consumer complaints about recurring mechanical problems, electrical malfunctions, and [severe safety defects](/blog/chevrolet-evs-recall-over-risk-of-bursting-into-flames). For some owners, these issues never seem to disappear - turning a once-exciting purchase into a costly and frustrating burden.

If your Chevrolet is constantly breaking down, you might be dealing with a lemon. While California’s [Chevrolet lemon laws](/manufacturers/chevrolet) protect consumers stuck with defective vehicles, determining whether your car qualifies and what steps to take next can be challenging.

At [CLINE APC](/), we’ve helped countless Chevy owners pursue compensation for their lemon vehicles. In this guide, we’ll go over the most common signs of a lemon Chevrolet and the steps you should take if your vehicle qualifies.

## Common Signs That Your Chevrolet Might Be a Lemon

California law considers a vehicle a lemon if:

- A significant defect affects its safety, value, or function.

- The issue first appeared while the vehicle was under warranty.

- The manufacturer or dealership made multiple unsuccessful repair attempts (at least two for major safety defects or four for other issues).

- The vehicle has spent 30 or more days in the shop for warranty repairs.

- The problem isn’t due to misuse or neglect by the owner.

Even if your Chevy doesn’t meet all of these criteria, ongoing mechanical failures that the dealership can’t seem to fix may still qualify it as a lemon.

Here’s what to watch out for.

## 1. Persistent Transmission Problems

Transmission failures are among the most commonly reported signs of a lemon Chevrolet. Owners of models like the Silverado, Equinox, and Cruze have described problems such as:

- Sluggish acceleration or sudden lurching.

- Hard shifts that feel like a sudden jolt.

- Unpredictable shifting into neutral while driving.

GM has issued multiple recalls related to transmission defects, including a [2022 recall](https://www.reuters.com/business/autos-transportation/gm-recall-more-than-461000-vehicles-over-transmission-issues-nhtsa-says-2024-11-13/#:~:text=The%20recall%20involves%20some%20models,the%20risk%20of%20a%20crash.) affecting Silverado and Tahoe trucks. However, a recall doesn’t always fix the issue - if your Chevy has undergone repairs but still experiences transmission problems, consider getting in touch with a Chevrolet lemon law attorney to discuss your options.

## 2. Electrical System Malfunctions

Modern Chevys are built with advanced electronic systems, but like any technology, they can malfunction. A few of the most frequently reported problems include:

- Touchscreen and infotainment issues

- Power window and lock failures

- Mysterious battery drain

- Warning lights that won’t turn off

These troubles can be frustrating and, in some cases, compromise safety. If they persist despite multiple trips to the dealership, your Chevy may be a lemon.

## 3. Steering and Suspension Issues

A car that doesn’t handle properly can make driving a nerve-wracking experience. Chevrolet models like the [Suburban](https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/rcl/2021/RCSB-21V455-1357.pdf) have been flagged for steering problems, with some owners reporting a sudden loss of power steering while on the road.

A failing steering system reduces your ability to control the vehicle, increasing [the risk of accidents](/blog/what-are-common-causes-of-car-accidents-in-los-angeles). Given that these defects directly affect a car’s ability to function safely, they may qualify for Chevrolet lemon law protection.

## 4. Repeated Brake System Failures

Brake issues are one of the most dangerous problems a vehicle can have. Chevrolet has recalled many models due to issues like:

- Delayed braking response

- Grinding or squeaking noises that persist after multiple repairs

- A brake pedal that fails to engage properly

A braking system that remains unreliable after multiple service visits could put your Chevy in lemon territory.

## 5. Engine Trouble

Engine failure usually leads to a cascade of other problems. Certain models, like the Chevrolet Malibu, have a well-documented history of excessive oil consumption, forcing owners to refill their oil far more often than expected.

More severe cases involve sudden stalling, where the engine shuts off while driving. The 2014-2017 Chevrolet Equinox, for example, has [defective piston rings](https://topclassactions.com/lawsuit-settlements/consumer-products/auto-news/gm-class-action-claims-chevy-equinox-gmc-terrain-vehicles-have-faulty-engines/comment-page-13/?wg-choose-original=false) that can cause oil leaks and eventual engine failure.

Lemon law covers engine defects because they pose a serious safety risk. Speak with a qualified Chevrolet lemon law attorney to determine whether you have a valid lemon law claim.

## What to Do if You Think Your Chevrolet is a Lemon?

Finding out your new Chevy is a lemon can be extremely frustrating. Thankfully, California’s lemon laws protect car buyers in this situation. Remember to act quickly, though - you have [just one year](/blog/big-news-governor-gavin-newsom-signs-new-ca-lemon-law-bill-despite-reservations) after the warranty expires to file your claim under current CA lemon laws.

## 1. Document Everything

Keep detailed documentation of all service visits, repairs, and communications with the dealership or manufacturer. Be sure to save:

- Work orders and invoices from each repair attempt.

- Notes on conversations with service advisors or customer support.

- Dates your vehicle was in the shop and how long it stayed there.

Having this evidence on hand will make it easier to prove your case if the manufacturer refuses to cooperate.

## 2. Give the Manufacturers a Final Repair Attempt

Before taking legal action, the manufacturer must be given a reasonable number of attempts to fix the issue. In California, this means at least two repair attempts for major safety defects or four for other issues.

## 3. File the Lemon Law Claim

If the issue continues or the dealership refuses to fix it, filing a lemon law claim may be the next step. You can escalate your case through the [California Department of Consumer Affairs (DCA)](https://www.dca.ca.gov/) or the [Better Business Bureau’s Auto Line program](https://bbbprograms.org/programs/all-programs/bbb-autoline).

Under CA lemon law, owners of lemon vehicles are [entitled to](/blog/the-components-of-a-lemon-law-refund-in-california):

- A lemon law buyback

- Coverage for all legal costs and attorney fees

- Compensation for incidental costs, towing fees, and other related expenses

While you can file a Chevrolet lemon law claim yourself, it’s always better to get legal representation. Here’s why.

## Why Hire a Chevrolet Lemon Law Lawyer?

Chevrolet and other big-name automakers often try to delay, deny, or minimize valid lemon law claims. Manufacturers may insist that the issue isn’t serious enough, claim the repairs were inadequate, or drag out the process to frustrate consumers into giving up.

An attorney can help level the playing field. Many offer free consultations and work on a contingency basis, meaning you don’t pay anything out-of-pocket. Their fees are typically included in the manufacturer’s settlement.

An experienced Chevrolet lemon law lawyer can:

- Review your case to determine if your Chevy meets lemon law criteria.

- Gather the necessary evidence, including repair orders, service history, and manufacturer communications.

- Negotiate with Chevrolet to push for a fair resolution, whether that’s a buyback, replacement, or cash settlement.

- Represent you in arbitration or court if the manufacturer refuses to cooperate.

Manufacturers have legal teams working to protect their bottom line - you should have someone fighting for your rights, too. If you’ve followed all the right steps and Chevy still won’t honor your lemon law case, legal help could be the key to getting the compensation you deserve.

